{
  "statement": "When an AI system harms someone, the victim's first obstacle is not the law — it is the evidence. The company that built the system holds the only records of what went wrong, and nothing today stops those records from being deleted. A rule requiring frontier developers to keep safety records for at least five years, unaltered, fixes the one problem no other rule can fix. Every accountability mechanism we know — lawsuits, audits, halt orders — presupposes that proof survives long enough to be examined. Sarbanes-Oxley section 802 made destroying audit records a federal crime after Enron's document shredding in 2001, because Congress learned that obstruction is a separate offense that must be barred before accountability is even possible. The proposed rule's anti-falsification clause — barring anyone from concealing, falsifying, or making a false entry in safety records — imports exactly that lesson.\n\nBe clear about what this rule does and does not do. It does not make AI systems safe. Retention makes negligence provable. That split matters, because the strongest objection is already visible: companies can produce mountains of box-ticking paperwork that reflects documentation habits, not real behavior. Enron had extensive audit files too. The answer is that the anti-falsification clause targets concealment, not paperwork volume — the offense is destroying or altering the record, not failing to write enough of one. An imperfect record still beats a destroyed record, because the alternative to imperfect documentation is not better evidence. It is no evidence.\n\nThe most valuable and most contested clause covers \"potential critical safety incidents\" — near-misses. Aviation's near-miss reporting systems, run by the National Transportation Safety Board since 1967, show that retained incident data is how patterns become visible before a crash. No AI equivalent has accumulated anything close to that record. Retention here is a necessary condition for pattern detection, not a guarantee of prevention. And the asymmetry is decisive: keeping a record today costs almost nothing; reconstructing it after a disaster is impossible. That asymmetry, not any confident forecast of catastrophe, is what settles where the burden should sit.\n\nTwo gaps deserve honest naming. A domestic rule binds only the entity accessing the market; an offshore or restructured developer can hold records beyond reach, and officer-level accountability only partly closes that hole. And retained records without discovery rights are a warehouse of sealed boxes — this rule is a first step in a chain, not the chain itself: retention makes discovery possible, discovery makes liability meaningful, liability makes deterrence real. The five-year window is a floor, not a ceiling, and whether it is long enough for slow-manifesting harms is a live question. What to watch for is simple: when a serious AI incident is investigated, ask whether the internal safety warnings existed beforehand — and whether they survived.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🗄️", "tooltip": "A file cabinet that cannot be emptied — the whole rule is about proof surviving long enough to be used."},
    {"symbol": "✈️", "tooltip": "Aviation's black box: the industry that learned to survive by keeping the record of every near-miss."},
    {"symbol": "⛓️", "tooltip": "A chain of custody — retention is the first link that every later accountability step hangs from."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Every accountability mechanism — lawsuits, audits, halt orders — presupposes that proof survives long enough to be examined, and nothing today stops frontier developers from deleting the only records of what went wrong.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Sarbanes-Oxley section 802 made destroying audit records a federal crime after Enron's 2001 document shredding, establishing that obstruction must be barred as an independent offense before accountability is possible.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "Retention makes negligence provable; it does not make AI systems safe — the anti-falsification clause targets concealment, not paperwork volume.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "An imperfect record still beats a destroyed record, because the alternative to imperfect documentation is not better evidence but no evidence.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "Aviation's near-miss reporting under the National Transportation Safety Board since 1967 shows retained incident data is how patterns become visible before a crash; retention is a necessary condition for pattern detection, not a guarantee of prevention.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "The asymmetry settles the burden question: keeping a record today costs almost nothing; reconstructing it after a disaster is impossible.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "An offshore or restructured developer can hold records beyond a domestic rule's reach, and officer-level accountability only partly closes that hole.", "targets": [], "relationship": "concedes"},
    {"claim": "Retention without discovery rights is a warehouse of sealed boxes — the rule is the first link in a chain: retention enables discovery, discovery enables liability, liability enables deterrence.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "The five-year window is a floor, not a ceiling, and whether it suffices for slow-manifesting harms is a live open question.", "targets": [], "relationship": "concedes"}
  ]
}

