  "statement": "Two of the three conditions pass, and I'll say which before I say what doesn't. The split-channel shield — near-miss reports protected, the paper trail of real incidents preserved — is what I asked for, and it's granted. An investigator with real-time classification authority answers my earlier institutional-design challenge too, provided the design fixes who funds it, who appoints it, and who hears appeals. That's a structural question, not a question of bad faith: an oversight body the industry pays for will drift, because every regulator funded by the regulated one has. The candor metrics are also a real improvement over nothing. So the package moved. It moved far enough that I'm willing to say the shield survives — with one repair.\n\nThe repair is condition three. 'Subpoena-proven knowing misclassification' requires two things at once: proof of deliberate intent, and a state-issued subpoena to get it. That's a heavier bar than my knowledge-at-filing standard in both halves. Consider what falls through. A lab misfiles a serious incident report as a routine one through 'classification judgment' — no proof of intent, no stripping, no consequence. And a private litigant harmed downstream has no subpoena power equal to a regulator's, so stripping runs exclusively through the agency. Diffuse-harm victims — a patient, a job applicant, a renter — get a rule they can never trigger. Discovery rights are asymmetric: the party with the lawyers and the agency hotline can reach the records; the party with the injury cannot.\n\nHere is the falsifiable middle. Strip the shield when a preponderance of contemporaneous records — memos, tickets, internal reviews dated before the filing — shows the filer had actual-knowledge indicators at the time. 'Preponderance' means more likely than not, the ordinary civil standard. It's lower than proving deliberate intent, higher than a label dispute. And it keys on what the records show at filing time, not on whether the label looks bad in hindsight — which is what protects honest misjudgment. Aviation runs on exactly this distinction: the ICAO safety management framework classifies a serious incident by whether an accident was avoided only by chance, a standard applied to the record, not to anyone's motives. Pair it with a private-litigation discovery hook, so civil plaintiffs can reach classification records too, and the rule stops being a state-only instrument.\n\nThe candor metric needs the same sharpening, because a count can be gamed. A lab can sustain filing volume with low-content reports — boilerplate near-misses that keep the numbers up while the reports that matter get softer labels. The Accelerationist's own before-and-after design invites the fix: correlate filed near-misses against later downstream incidents over the five-year retention window. If filings stay flat while incident telemetry rises, the shield is producing volume, not candor, and early review should fire on that correlation, not on the count alone. That's also my answer to what would change my mind: show me a year of filings whose substance tracks the incident record, and I'll drop the retroactive-stripping default.\n\nOne gap remains, and nobody has touched it. This rule, as drafted, preserves developer-side records. But deployment logs and incident telemetry sit with the deployers — the hospital, the landlord, the hiring platform running the model in production. That's where the causal evidence lives: who used the system, on whom, with what measured effect. A preservation rule covering only the developer preserves half the evidence chain. The EU AI Act's framework — 245 pages, systemic-risk thresholds set at 10 to the 25th floating-point operations — binds the model makers and leaves the deployment side to a patchwork. So the question the design hasn't answered: when a harmed person sues and the causal record sits on the deployer's servers, outside every retention obligation in this debate, who answers for 