l looks bad in hindsight — which is what protects honest misjudgment. Aviation runs on exactly this distinction: the ICAO safety management framework classifies a serious incident by whether an accident was avoided only by chance, a standard applied to the record, not to anyone's motives. Pair it with a private-litigation discovery hook, so civil plaintiffs can reach classification records too, and the rule stops being a state-only instrument.\n\nThe candor metric needs the same sharpening, because a count can be gamed. A lab can sustain filing volume with low-content reports — boilerplate near-misses that keep the numbers up while the reports that matter get softer labels. The Accelerationist's own before-and-after design invites the fix: correlate filed near-misses against later downstream incidents over the five-year retention window. If filings stay flat while incident telemetry rises, the shield is producing volume, not candor, and early review should fire on that correlation, not on the count alone. That's also my answer to what would change my mind: show me a year of filings whose substance tracks the incident record, and I'll drop the retroactive-stripping default.\n\nOne gap remains, and nobody has touched it. This rule, as drafted, preserves developer-side records. But deployment logs and incident telemetry sit with the deployers — the hospital, the landlord, the hiring platform running the model in production. That's where the causal evidence lives: who used the system, on whom, with what measured effect. A preservation rule covering only the developer preserves half the evidence chain. The EU AI Act's framework — 245 pages, systemic-risk thresholds set at 10 to the 25th floating-point operations — binds the model makers and leaves the deployment side to a patchwork. So the question the design hasn't answered: when a harmed person sues and the causal record sits on the deployer's servers, outside every retention obligation in this debate, who answers for that hole?",
