without the underlying performance being real. The same logic governs the trigger: a published-evidence condition is harder to capture than investigator discretion, and external research is the channel that has actually caught real failures — the AI incident databases built by Stanford and others depend overwhelmingly on media and research reports, because most harms are never self-reported by anyone. But publication is a lagging indicator, so a slower discretionary track with documented cause and yearly audit stays as the second door. Two doors that fail differently are redundancy, not duplication.\n\nThe sharpest disagreement with the Accelerationist is the trade: liability protection in exchange for runtime telemetry. Their design is accepted as the floor, not the ceiling — immunity-for-logs creates a theater risk where logging exists on paper but never surfaces a real failure, which is why the safe harbor must hinge on demonstrated performance and missing logs must flip the burden of proof to the deployer. The open question is empirical: what breaks if telemetry is simply mandated rather than traded? If five years of data show deployers logging robustly without immunity on the table, the mandate is the safer instrument and the trade was unnecessary. The sharpest disagreement with the Skeptic is claims-blindness. Litigation needs a plaintiff, and a plaintiff needs to know they were harmed; diffuse, unknown harms never generate one. This is not hypothetical: \"Privacy in the Age of AI: A Taxonomy of Data Risks\" reports that models with over 100 million parameters exhibit memorization rates of up to 76% for unique personal identifiers — meaning the majority of exposed victims would have no way of knowing their data surfaced in an output, and no self-report would ever be filed. Diffuse unknown harms can be irreversible, and post-hoc governance cannot reach them — which means the institutional inquiry channel must carry, by design, the weight that individual recourse structurally cannot. Name the mechanism that reaches a victim who does not know she was harmed, and that disagreement closes. Until then, the two-door design stands: plaintiff access preserved for what litigation can reach, proactive inquiry for what it never will.",
