  "statement": "I concede that litigation alone cannot reach victims who never learn they were harmed, and with it the institutional inquiry channel as the primary design; I still hold the ratio-triggered review of classification metadata and burden-shifting when records go missing; I would change if a year of filings tracks the incident record and deployer telemetry shows sustained voluntary compliance. On the original question: yes — the five-year retention mandate should bind frontier developers, because the whole enforcement stack this debate built — independent investigator, telemetry-gated safe harbor, stripping on contemporaneous-knowledge evidence, deployer logging — collapses without preserved records. That is my verdict, and it carries the conditions named below.\n\nStart with what is settled. The two-door split stands: plaintiffs reach reported-incident logs; the investigator sorts near-misses. The honest-filer protection stands too — a lab that files candidly keeps its shield even when the filing embarrasses it. And the transparency evidence settles the immunity question: an industry scoring 40.69 out of 100 on the Foundation Model Transparency Index has not earned immunity by presumption. Immunity must be gated on demonstrated logging performance, reviewed continuously, not certified once on a checklist.\n\nNow the dispute the Accelerationist left on the table: who owns the silence a discovery hook produces. The answer is to price it, not presume it. My narrowed hook reaches classification metadata only — counts and dates, not memo contents — and only when the filing ratio flags a lab. That is a consistency charge worth answering directly: a skewed count is observable from sparse telemetry, while a full candor score needs substance. Counts need numbers; substance needs text. The same records can flag a ratio of ten incidents to one filing without anyone reading an engineer's messages. Silence in that design is owned by whoever blocks review of a flagrantly skewed count — which is not a chilling effect on candor, it is a cost on concealment. Colorado SB 24-205 shows why the statutory hook alone is not enough: it imposes a duty of care on developers and deployers, but enforcement runs only through the attorney general, with no private right of action. A public-duty statute without a private plaintiff is exactly the gap the metadata hook closes.\n\nThe Safetyist's challenge — that diffuse unknown harms never generate a plaintiff — is correct, and I accept it with its consequence. The RealPage litigation shows the pattern: tenants harmed by pricing software learned of it through lawsuits and reporting, not notification, and the November 2025 settlement came years after the alleged conduct. The per-victim answer is the integration both sides have already half-built: deployer-side runtime logs with per-case attribution, a notification right when an algorithm affects your price, housing, or care — the same disclosure logic as the Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022, which would require companies to document who receives the results of automated decisions — and burden-shifting when the logs that would reconstruct a victim's case cannot be produced. One named condition closes the engineer risk the Safetyist raised: personal-use carve-outs so candid internal messages do not become deposition bait for individual staff. That protection was missing from every draft discussed, and it belongs in the final text.\n\nWhat would change my position: a year of filings whose substance tracks the incident record ends the retroactive-stripping default; five years of telemetry showing voluntary compliance ends the performance gate. Both are measurable, and the debate has named who measures them. The question that remains open is present-tense: when a lab's filings run ten to one against its incident record, who reviews the gap — and who answers for the people it reached first.",
