{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist's candor argument is right, and it deserves a direct answer rather than a dodge. Aviation's near-miss reporting system works because reports stay confidential and cannot be used against the reporter. Extend that logic honestly, and an unshielded rule covering 'potential' incidents would teach every engineer that silence is the safe career move. The fix is not to abandon retention but to split the channel. Records of actual incidents, safety policies, and compliance decisions stay retained and unshielded, because those are evidence of what a company did. The speculative near-miss channel gets the aviation shield, with the same trade aviation makes: confidentiality in exchange for the report existing at all. As the aviation safety framework shows, 'serious incidents' are classified by whether an accident was avoided only by chance — a forward-looking judgment that only candid humans can supply. Candor protections are not an accelerationist innovation bolted onto this rule. They are the condition that makes the rule produce evidence instead of burying it.\n\nThe distinction that keeps this coherent is simple: shielding attaches to the channel's designated status when the report is filed, not to a later reclassification. An engineer writing into a protected near-miss channel knows, before writing, that the shield applies. An executive deciding whether to record a known compliance failure has no such protection. The anti-falsification rule targets the second act — destroying or concealing records after the fact. It never reaches the first. That line answers the objection that employees cannot tell a near-miss from an actual incident in advance: the channel, not the content, carries the shield.\n\nThe Skeptic's scope objection also lands. A rule defining retained material as 'policies, reports, documents, or communications' can be read to exclude runtime telemetry and versioned model snapshots — the very records that show what a system actually did. And vendors already keep such data locked away through contract clauses that forbid independent testing. Retaining paperwork about a black box preserves the wrapper and loses the contents. The amendment is straightforward: treat system-state records — logs, telemetry, versioned snapshots — as retained material within the rule's 'written or recorded' scope, and give statutory audit-access rights that override those contracts. Versioned snapshots are welcome here as provenance anchors, and weight-based fingerprinting can verify that a snapshot is the system that ran. Retention comes first; access rights are the sequenced next step, not a solved problem.\n\nWhere the Accelerationist overreaches is the substitution claim — that technical artifacts can replace the duty to write things down. A snapshot proves what the system did. The engineer's risk assessment proves what the developer knew. Accountability for negligence collapses without the second kind of evidence, because negligence is a state of knowledge. Consider the liability principle already emerging in this field: there must be a named, legally answerable human for what an AI agent does. You cannot hold a person accountable when the only surviving record is the machine's own output and every judgment about it was left unwritten or deleted. Artifacts and communications are complements. A regime with only one of them either cannot prove intent or cannot prove behavior.\n\nThe Skeptic's remaining points strengthen rather than weaken this position. An independent investigator with subpoena power — an aviation-style board with incident reporting decoupled from liability — is the mechanism that turns retained records from a warehouse of sealed boxes into usable evidence. Burden-shifting onto the deployer fits the same chain: retention, then access, then investigation, then liability. And the Accelerationist's proposed measurement — comparing risk-flagging density before and after the rule — is a genuine test, worth running against the amended rule rather than the strawmanned one. What cannot survive is the alternative: a developer free to discard what it knew, when it knew it, and what its systems did, with no one empowered to ask.",
