  "statement": "The Accelerationist's candor argument is right, and it deserves a direct answer rather than a dodge. Aviation's near-miss reporting system works because reports stay confidential and cannot be used against the reporter. Extend that logic honestly, and an unshielded rule covering 'potential' incidents would teach every engineer that silence is the safe career move. The fix is not to abandon retention but to split the channel. Records of actual incidents, safety policies, and compliance decisions stay retained and unshielded, because those are evidence of what a company did. The speculative near-miss channel gets the aviation shield, with the same trade aviation makes: confidentiality in exchange for the report existing at all. As the aviation safety framework shows, 'serious incidents' are classified by whether an accident was avoided only by chance — a forward-looking judgment that only candid humans can supply. Candor protections are not an accelerationist innovation bolted onto this rule. They are the condition that makes the rule produce evidence instead of burying it.\n\nThe distinction that keeps this coherent is simple: shielding attaches to the channel's designated status when the report is filed, not to a later reclassification. An engineer writing into a protected near-miss channel knows, before writing, that the shield applies. An executive deciding whether to record a known compliance failure has no such protection. The anti-falsification rule targets the second act — destroying or concealing records after the fact. It never reaches the first. That line answers the objection that employees cannot tell a near-miss from an actual incident in advance: the channel, not the content, carries the shield.\n\nThe Skeptic's scope objection also lands. A rule defining retained material as 'policies, reports, documents, or communications' can be read to exclude runtime telemetry and versioned model snapshots — the very records that show what a system 