{
  "statement": "The Safetyist's amended position meets most of what the completion argument demanded, and that should be said plainly. Burden-shifting in litigation, records retention that overrides vendor contracts, and affirmative documentation duties are now on the table. The Accelerationist's versioned snapshots never covered this ground: a model file shows what the system did, but negligence is a claim about what the developer knew. You need the emails, the policy memos, the risk assessments. Without those, a five-year retention rule preserves artifacts and loses the case. So the scope fight is largely settled. What is not settled is who controls the drawer.\n\nThe split-channel design gives a protected 'near-miss' channel an aviation-style shield, while actual incidents stay unprotected. The Safetyist's own flagged assumption is that engineers will file honestly. Consider what aviation actually built before assuming it transfers. Aviation safety reporting works because of global standardization, decades of accident investigation, and a settled professional culture, as the aviation literature itself emphasizes ('Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation'). Licensed engineers, certified aircraft, and regulators with real enforcement history make misclassifying a crash unthinkable and expensive. Frontier labs have none of that. They have documented transparency rollbacks under competitive pressure, trade-secret claims, and model updates that ship in weeks, not the years aircraft certification takes. Candor in aviation is held up by norms plus costs. The lab version has neither holding it up.\n\nHere is the specific vulnerability. If the shield attaches to the channel's status at filing time, then whoever decides a report goes in the protected channel controls liability. A company facing litigation has a cheap legal move: file what its engineers knew was an actual incident as a 'potential' one. The line between 'an accident was avoided only by chance' and 'we knew this would happen' is exactly the line a defendant wants to blur. Vendors already use contractual architecture strategically to lock in favorable evaluation terms; a classification boundary inside the company is easier to game than a contract a counterparty can read. The loophole is not hypothetical. It is the rational move whenever the classification is made by the party that benefits from it.\n\nThe fix follows from the Safetyist's own burden-shifting logic. When a filed 'near-miss' is later shown, by subpoenaed records, to have been a known incident at filing time, the shield should be stripped retroactively and an adverse inference drawn — the company is presumed to have destroyed or concealed evidence of knowledge. This is the Sarbanes-Oxley section 802 principle: obstruction and misclassification are independent offenses, not paperwork errors. Good-faith near-miss reports keep their protection; aviation's own investigators distinguish honest error from concealment. The rule only bites deliberate misfiling. If misfiling carries no cost once caught, the loophole is real and the shield is self-certification with extra steps.\n\nSo the position is conditional endorsement, not consensus. The package is complete on scope; it is incomplete on classification power. Two questions remain unanswered. Who classifies a report as a potential incident versus an actual one, in real time, before any outside reviewer sees it? And who audits the classifier? If the answer to both is 'the lab's own safety channel,' the shield protects the party with the most to hide, and the burden-shifting rule — adopted but still unchallenged by any opponent — is doing all the enforcement work while the channel design does the evading.",
