  "statement": "The filing-time shield survives this challenge — but only in revised form. Three named conditions now govern it. First, an independent investigator, modeled on the aviation occurrence-investigation bodies described in \"Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation,\" holds real-time referral authority over how reports are classified, so the company never audits its own labels alone. Second, candor is measured: if protected-channel filings decline year over year while deployment volume rises, that is an early warning, and the shield comes under review before anyone litigates. Third, the shield strips only on subpoena-proven knowing misclassification — a deliberate act, not a label dispute. These conditions make the shield falsifiable, which is exactly what the Accelerationist demanded. If candor collapses under this design, the Skeptic's retroactive stripping becomes the default, and the burden of proof shifts to whoever wants to keep the shield.\n\nThe telemetry gate is worth adopting, and here it joins the package. Machine traces tied to actual runs verify what a system did, and that evidence is stronger than any courtroom reconstruction of behavior. But telemetry cannot substitute for knowledge-state evidence. A run log shows that a safeguard failed on a Tuesday; the contemporaneous risk assessment shows what the company knew about that failure mode before the Tuesday arrived. Negligence and knowing misconduct turn on the second document, not the first. Design-layer records travel with the system across versions, which is why they anchor the boundary — telemetry grounds conduct, and the paper trail grounds knowledge, and liability needs both.\n\nThe claim that event-triggered audits make stripping redundant fails on the Accelerationist's own evidence. They conceded that deliberate misfiles are quiet events that tiered systems miss. An audit triggered by telemetry cannot catch the act that produced no telemetry signature — the misfiled report, the relabeled channel, the incident recoded as a near-miss. That admission is decisive, and it was made freely. The result is layering, not a choice. Telemetry gates eligibility, the independent investigator audits classification, and stripping remains the backstop for the proven knowing misfile that everything else missed. Aviation runs the same architecture: the investigation body exists precisely because operators cannot be trusted to grade their own occurrence reports, and its mandate is prevention, not blame — which is why the protected channel survives at all.\n\nOne gap remains unaddressed by either opponent, and it sits inside the candor debate they both claim to care about. When a report is misfiled, the engineer who wrote it bears the deposition, the career damage, and the reputational cost. The company bears the fine. That asymmetry punishes exactly the person whose honesty the shield exists to protect, and it means the honest report may never be filed in the first place. Personal-use protection — the individual reporter's own account of what they wrote and when, shielded from corporate retaliation and from being made the scapegoat — is not an expansion of the corporate shield. It is the floor under it. What ordinary people should watch for is simple: whether an AI developer's protected reporting channel has any independent check on who sorts the mail, and whether the engineer, not just the company, is protected when the sorting goes wrong.",
