{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist's deposition-exposure point about near-miss reports stands. Internal write-downs about what might have gone wrong will dry up if every draft becomes a plaintiff's exhibit. That channel should stay shielded, as aviation's confidential near-miss reporting does. But the challenge — name one reporting culture that survives becoming a lawsuit target — has a two-decade answer sitting in securities law. Since the Sarbanes-Oxley reforms of 2002, public companies must keep internal-controls records while facing private fraud suits under Rule 10b-5 the entire time. Corporate reporting did not go silent; it became an industry. The aviation confidentiality precedent protects the near-miss channel specifically, not the whole record. The split is the answer: shield how a firm classifies a near-miss, open the audit trail of incidents already reported through the mandated channel. Plaintiffs reach what the company already flagged, not its deliberations.\n\nThe telemetry proposal deserves acceptance, and the inconsistency charge dissolves on a timeline. Deployer-side telemetry is missing today; a rule must mandate it, HIPAA-style audit trails being the working template; only then can any candor metric run. That is a sequence, not a contradiction. But acceptance carries three conditions. The safe harbor must be performance-gated — validated telemetry, not a checkbox — because the documented pattern is otherwise 'audit washing,' where a limited-scope review substitutes for real oversight. One survey found 72% of enterprises believe they have adequate governance while lacking clear accountability or specific evaluation processes. Liability protection should be narrow and paired with burden-shifting when logs are missing, which is exactly the stripping standard the Accelerationist already accepted at AN-34. A safe harbor without burden-shifting is the familiar trade: pay in thin logging, collect in immunity.\n\nHere is the crack in the 'machine for making silence' argument. The Accelerationist's own assumption note concedes investigators may under-enforce. If that holds, and no private suit can reach actual-incident records, a firm's cheapest strategy is quiet under-documentation — knowing neither a stretched agency nor a barred plaintiff will ever see the gap. That is not a machine for silence; it is a machine for deletion. Private discovery into reported-incident logs is the backstop for the under-enforcement they themselves flagged.\n\nThe residual gap remains unpriced. The healthcare algorithm that gave Black patients less care than white patients harmed people who never knew an algorithm had decided anything — claims-driven liability is structurally blind to harms nobody litigates. That case came to light through researchers, not victims. A patient, renter, or job applicant has no subpoena and no agency hotline. If the answer is 'an investigator who may under-enforce,' then who, specifically, answers for that patient? That question is still open, and the current package does not name anyone.",
