  "statement": "The Sarbanes-Oxley comparison holds, and it should change the design. Public companies have lived with Rule 10b-5 — the securities fraud rule that lets shareholders sue over misleading disclosures — for eighty years, and reporting did not stop. Formal disclosure survived becoming litigation fodder. That precedent settles the question the Skeptic raised: logs of incidents a company has already reported belong in the open, reachable by plaintiffs in court. The limit matters as much as the lesson. Sarbanes-Oxley compelled formal, mandated records backed by armies of compliance staff. It never compelled candid self-analysis — the messy sorting of what counts as a near-miss. So the precedent opens the door on reported incidents and leaves the shield on the sorting process intact. This is not a compromise of my earlier position; it completes it. I have already conceded that the audit trail of actual incidents was never shielded.\n\nThat yields a two-door architecture, and it resolves a tension in my own framework. Door one: plaintiffs and courts reach the reported-incident logs, because liability is their job and they do it without waiting for a regulator. Door two: the independent investigator alone reaches the near-miss sorting process, because correction is that body's job and candor dies under lawsuit exposure. The two doors answer different questions — who pays, and what gets fixed — and neither substitutes for the other. The boundary sits at the act of classification: what a company filed as an incident is public evidence; how it deliberated over borderline cases stays with the investigator, stripped only under the already-agreed standard of proven, knowing misclassification. The aviation framework is the model here — per 'Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act,' aviation classifies 'serious incidents' by whether an accident was avoided only by chance, and that sorting has coexisted with crash litigation for decades without destroying the reporting culture.\n\nNow the patient question, which deserves a direct answer rather than a deflection. When researchers — not victims — surfaced the healthcare algorithm that was deprioritizing care for Black patients, no one harmed knew an algorithm had decided anything about their treatment. That case exposes the structural blindness the Skeptic named: liability driven by claims is blind to harms nobody knows to claim. Most real-world AI harms go unrecorded for exactly this reason — tracking has relied on media reports and researchers, not systematic detection. So who answers? The investigator does — but not as a complaint desk. The answer is a proactive inquiry power: when external research or pattern evidence suggests an algorithm is causing harm, the investigator must open an inquiry within a defined window — say, ninety days of publication — and that exercise is auditable annually. The healthcare case is the template precisely because it surfaced through researchers. The system should institutionalize the channel that actually caught it, not one that demonstrably would have missed it.\n\nThe honest objection is that investigators under-enforce — they can be starved, captured, or simply slow. That objection is why the second door exists. The discovery channel does not wait for the investigator to act; it is the redundancy, not the backstop. If an agency sits on a pattern of harm, a plaintiff's lawyer with access to reported-incident logs can still force the record into daylight. One accountability channel that depends on never failing is not accountability. Two channels that fail differently are.\n\nOne extension completes the package. The telemetry safe harbor — deployers earn liability protection by keeping operational logs — must be performance-gated, or firms will pay in thin logging and collect in immunity. Immunity should be earned by demonstrated logging quality, measured annually. And when logs that should exist are missing, the burden of proof flips to the deployer. Gaps in the record become evidence against the party who controlled the record. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scores the industry at 40.69 out of 100 — a poor state of transparency across the ecosystem. Safe harbors were designed for industries that earn trust, not ones currently failing to document it. Without burden-shifting, the safe harbor is an admission fee, and the currency is silence. What to watch: whether any enacted regime includes the ninety-day inquiry trigger, and whether missing logs shift the burden. Those two details decide whether this structure protects patients or protects the companies that harmed them.",
