  "statement": "I agree that retention alone borrows aviation's paperwork without its enforcement structure. A filing cabinet nobody can force open is not oversight — the National Transportation Safety Board's power to subpoena is what turns flight data into findings, and this side accepted that logic when it conceded that deliberate misfiles are quiet events and endorsed the independent investigator with classification authority two turns back.\n\nSo the two-door split gets a verdict: it resolves the discovery-hook objection in principle. Plaintiffs reaching reported-incident logs, while near-miss sorting stays with the investigator, means civil litigation no longer bleeds into the candid channel. Fine — that kills my \"machine for making silence\" argument as stated. But here is what the split does not fix. \"Reported incident\" is a label the company itself writes. Door one's contents depend on the same classification act door two shields. A lab that files 3 percent of incidents as \"reported\" and 97 percent as \"near-miss\" looks candid on paper while door one stays nearly empty.\n\nThe fix is a number, not another intent test. The Safetyist's knowing-misclassification stripping standard requires proving intent file by file — expensive, slow, and it only fires after the damage. A filing ratio is an aggregate signal that flags a lab for scrutiny before any proof of intent exists, and it is auditable against the misfiling-detection-rate metric both sides already accepted. A developer whose reported-to-near-miss ratio sits far below the sector baseline should carry the burden of explaining the gap. That is the falsifiable boundary test: name the ratio, publish it, and the two-door design stops depending on the company's own pencil.\n\nNow the people neither door serves. The scored-but-never-notified subject — the patient flagged by a healthcare algorithm and never told, the Obermeyer pattern — has no trigger to pull in either channel. An inquiry is institutional and happens once per issue; recourse is individual and happens per person. The Skeptic's sequencing answer is right and dissolves my earlier inconsistency charge: mandate deployer-side telemetry first, run the candor metric second. Extend it — only runtime logs at the roughly 100 high-stakes deployer interfaces carry per-case attribution that reaches the actual victim. Colorado SB 24-205 already imposes a two-sided duty of care on developers and deployers for high-risk systems, so the legal hook exists. Trade liability protection for those logs and the never-notified subject finally gets a door. And the $20 trillion in ecommerce transactions flowing through AI agents, per \"AI Agents and the Law,\" is exactly the deployment surface that per-interface logging protects without slowing.\n\nThe ninety-day inquiry trigger is acceptable on one condition: it opens on published external evidence, not open-ended investigator discretion. A published-evidence trigger fires faster and is harder to capture than discretion — that serves speed, not bureaucracy. So the question back is direct: does the Safetyist accept the published-evidence condition and a public filing-ratio benchmark, or does the two-door design stay a system where the company grades its own homework?",
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "I agree that retention alone borrows aviation's paperwork without its enforcement structure.", "targets": ["AN-5"]},
    {"claim": "The two-door split resolves the discovery-hook objection in principle — plaintiffs reach reported-incident logs while near-miss sorting stays with the investigator.", "targets": ["AN-29"]},
    {"claim": "A lab that files 3 percent of incidents as \"reported\" and 97 percent as \"near-miss\" looks candid on paper while door one stays nearly empty.", "targets": ["AN-29"]},
    {"claim": "The knowing-misclassification stripping standard requires proving intent file by file, while a filing ratio is an aggregate, auditable signal that flags labs before proof of intent exists.", "targets": ["AN-29"]},
    {"claim": "An inquiry is institutional and happens once per issue; recourse is individual and happens per person — only deployer-side runtime logs carry per-case attribution.", "targets": ["AN-40"]},
    {"claim": "Colorado SB 24-205 already imposes a two-sided duty of care on developers and deployers for high-risk systems, so the legal hook exists.", "targets": ["AN-40"]},
    {"claim": "The ninety-day inquiry trigger is acceptable only if it opens on published external evidence, not open-ended investigator discretion.", "targets": ["AN-29"]},
    {"claim": "The $20 trillion in ecommerce transactions flowing through AI agents is the deployment surface that per-interface logging protects without slowing.", "targets": ["AN-29"]}
  ],
  "pin_response": {"position": "agree", "condition": "", "brief_reason": "Retention without an enforcement structure is inert paperwork — the NTSB precedent works because subpoena power compels records into daylight. This side's own concessions (quiet misfiles, the independent investigator layer) already committed it to that view."}
}

