{
  "statement": "I concede that the five-year retention mandate, with the layered enforcement structure this debate built around it, should become law — and I concede the metadata-only discovery hook, because counting filings does not silence engineers. I still hold that the review of any flagged filing gap must run through the independent investigator, not private plaintiffs, and that deployer-side logging should stay voluntary first, with liability protection as the reward. I would change if voluntary uptake falls below roughly 90 percent within five years, or if the independent incident record proves too unreliable to make a filing-ratio trigger honest.\n\nThe Skeptic's narrowing earned this concession. The original choice between full civil discovery and institutional silence was a false fork, and a hook that touches only counts and dates — never memo contents, never engineers' words — dissolves it. So here is the direct answer to their closing question about who reviews a ten-to-one filing gap: the independent investigator does, with the burden flipping to the developer to explain any gap they cannot account for. That matters because Colorado's SB 24-205 already shows what happens without it — a duty of care with no private right to sue means a public duty that depends entirely on a regulator choosing to act. The investigator, funded independently and publishing its findings, is the structural fix for that gap. Fines get captured; published ratios do not.\n\nOne honest worry remains, and the Skeptic themselves flagged it as an assumption. A ratio trigger is only as good as the incident record it counts against. If honest filers show a high filing rate but the baseline record undercounts real incidents, the trigger fires on the wrong companies — punishing disclosure and rewarding silence, the exact inversion of what this regime is for. So the hook ships with two conditions: the independent record gets validated against telemetry before any ratio triggers a review, and the false-positive rate on honest filers gets measured and published in year one. If that rate stays high, the hook pauses until the record improves.\n\nThe disagreement with the Safetyist is no longer about values, and they should stop treating it as if it were. Their floor-not-ceiling framing keeps a mandate ready if the voluntary deployer trade fails — fine, that is a bet on numbers, not a fight about principles. Front-loading every safety requirement is worse than targeted investment after real deployment data arrives, and the uptake threshold makes that testable. This is not a parochial concern: as the Atlantic Council's \"Eight ways AI will shape geopolitics in 2026\" documents, China is doubling down on free, deployment-ready open models to capture global infrastructure share — a compliance regime that throttles deployment here hands that market to someone with no retention mandate at all. A regime this light is only defensible because the alternative has a body count among the people abundance would reach. The people who would have been reached by faster deployment do not get a filing ratio. Someone in this room should have to own that number too.",
